### Declaring a Generic Class

A generic class is defined with a **type parameter section** delimited by angle brackets (`<T>`) immediately following the class name. The formal type parameter `T` serves as a placeholder for a concrete type specified by caller code:
```java
public class Box<T> {
    private T content;

    public void set(T content) {
        this.content = content;
    }

    public T get() {
        return content;
    }
}
```

---

### Standard Type Parameter Naming Conventions

By convention in Java software engineering, type parameter names are single, uppercase letters:
* **`E`**: Element (extensively used by the Java Collections Framework, e.g., `List<E>`, `Set<E>`)
* **`K`**: Key (used in maps, e.g., `Map<K, V>`)
* **`V`**: Value (used in maps, e.g., `Map<K, V>`)
* **`T`**: Type (general-purpose first type parameter)
* **`U`, `S`**: 2nd and 3rd general-purpose type parameters
* **`N`**: Number (used for numeric types)

---

### Multi-Type Containers: The Pair Pattern

Classes can accept multiple type parameters separated by commas. A classic example is the immutable `Pair<K, V>` tuple container:
```java
public class Pair<K, V> {
    private final K key;
    private final V value;

    public Pair(K key, V value) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
    }

    public K getKey() { return key; }
    public V getValue() { return value; }
}
```

---

### Critical Boundary: The Static Context Restriction

A universal rule in Java: **A static field or static method cannot reference a class-level type parameter `T`**.
```java
public class Box<T> {
    // COMPILE ERROR: Cannot make a static reference to the non-static type T
    private static T sharedItem;

    // COMPILE ERROR: Cannot make a static reference to the non-static type T
    public static T getShared() { return sharedItem; }
}
```
**Why?** Static members are shared across all instances of `Box`. If caller A creates `Box<String>` and caller B creates `Box<Integer>`, there is only ONE `Box.class` loaded in JVM memory. If `sharedItem` were static, what type would it hold? `String` or `Integer`? Because types are erased at runtime and static members belong to the class rather than instance, class-level type variables are strictly prohibited in static scopes.

---

